# XSH 2.7 XSI Interprocess Communication
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The rules the message queue, semaphore and shared memory functions share.
# The permission tests change user and group IDs: they need root, and each
# case runs in a child process that gives up privilege.

assertion  XSH.2.7-xsi-ipc.001
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.7
class      disputed
when       XSI
says       Message queue, semaphore set and shared memory identifiers returned
           by the three get functions are positive: ten identifiers of each
           kind made with IPC_PRIVATE are all greater than zero and pairwise
           different.
note       disputed (new, see the report): 2.7 calls each identifier a
           unique positive integer, while msgget(), semget() and shmget()
           say non-negative. Zero would break one of the two texts.
           Distinctness alone is XSH.msgget.001, XSH.semget.001 and
           XSH.shmget.001.
test       t/001-ids-positive.c

assertion  XSH.2.7-xsi-ipc.002
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.7
class      conditional
when       XSI
says       For a message queue whose owner and group are A and B and whose
           mode gives read and write to a chosen set of the user, group
           and other classes, a process with effective IDs (A, any) is
           judged by the user class alone, one with (not A, B) by the group
           class alone, and one with neither by the other class alone: of
           eight modes and three kinds of process, msgsnd() (write) and
           msgrcv() (read) are allowed exactly where the class that applies
           has the bit and fail with EACCES elsewhere.
needs      root
timeout    60
test       t/002-msg-class-rules.c

assertion  XSH.2.7-xsi-ipc.003
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.7
class      conditional
when       XSI
says       For a semaphore set the same holds: of eight modes and three
           kinds of process, semctl(GETVAL) (read) and semop() with a
           change of +1 (alter) are allowed exactly where the class that
           applies has the bit and fail with EACCES elsewhere.
needs      root
timeout    60
test       t/003-sem-class-rules.c

assertion  XSH.2.7-xsi-ipc.004
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.7
class      conditional
when       XSI
says       For a shared memory segment the same holds: of eight modes and
           three kinds of process, shmat() with SHM_RDONLY (read) and
           shmat() for reading and writing are allowed exactly where the
           class that applies has the bit(s) and fail with EACCES elsewhere.
needs      root
timeout    60
test       t/004-shm-class-rules.c

assertion  XSH.2.7-xsi-ipc.005
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.7
class      conditional
when       XSI
says       A process whose effective user ID equals the creator's ID of a
           message queue, not its owner's ID (the owner was changed with
           msgctl(IPC_SET)), is judged by the user class; likewise a process
           whose effective group ID is the creator's group, not the owner's
           group, is judged by the group class; and one that equals the
           new owner is judged by the user class too.
needs      root
timeout    60
test       t/005-msg-creator-ids.c

assertion  XSH.2.7-xsi-ipc.006
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.7
class      conditional
when       XSI
says       The time recorded by msgsnd (msg_stime), msgrcv (msg_rtime),
           msgctl IPC_SET (msg_ctime), semop (sem_otime), shmat
           (shm_atime) and shmdt (shm_dtime) is the whole second that the clock showed, not
           rounded up: called late in a second the value is not later than
           the CLOCK_REALTIME seconds read just after the call, and it is not
           earlier than those read just before.
note       Each call is made when the fraction of the second is between 0.8
           and 0.95 (waiting up to 1.2 s for it) so that a clock rounded
           to the nearest second would show up.
timeout    60
test       t/006-timestamps-floor.c
